<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    Lowest-level project: system marker interfaces, architecture attributes, and
    shape interfaces shared by Domain entities and their Application DTOs. Those
    shape interfaces may carry default-implemented members when a rule must have a
    single home across both implementers (IBurnSettingsInfo's early-entry clock) —
    so a little behaviour lives here, deliberately, not just declarations.
    THE ONE INVARIANT, restated for what it always meant: Base must stay the bottom of
    the graph, so every project it references must sit on a chain that TERMINATES.
    G5 lane 4b-iii A added the first and so far only ProjectReference —
    Humans.Users.Contracts — and the chain is
      Humans.Interfaces -> Humans.Users.Contracts -> Humans.Onboarding.Contracts -> nothing.
    A terminating chain cannot cycle, which is why lanes 3b (#1316) and 3c (#1318) spent
    themselves making Humans.Users.Contracts terminal before this reference was allowed.
    Do NOT add a reference to anything that is not on such a chain, and do NOT add an
    outbound reference to Humans.Users.Contracts or Humans.Onboarding.Contracts — either
    closes an assembly cycle through Base.

    EXTERNAL DEPENDENCIES ARE UNRESTRICTED — Peter's ruling, 2026-08-15. Base may take
    any NuGet package or shared framework it needs; only Humans.* ProjectReferences are
    forbidden. Earlier revisions of this comment claimed a "no-NuGet-packages promise"
    and described Base as framework-free. Both were false when written: this project has
    carried FrameworkReference Microsoft.AspNetCore.App since ISection landed, long
    before phase 3, and it now also carries CsvHelper, Serilog, Octokit, EF Core and
    Npgsql. Do not reason from package count when placing a type here; reason from the
    ProjectReference rule above and from whether the type is genuinely cross-section.

    NodaTime is here because IBurnSettingsInfo's calendar members are LocalDate and
    Instant. Namespaces intentionally remain Humans.Application.* (namespace ≠ assembly)
    so call sites and the Humans.Analyzers full-name constants are unaffected by the move.

    G5 lane 3a-1 (nobodies-collective/Humans#866) made this the Base fill: 60 generic files
    arrived from Humans.Application (34), Humans.Domain (16) and Humans.Infrastructure (10) —
    the configuration registry, CacheKeys, the CSV seam, the formatting/timing extensions,
    the cross-cutting cache interfaces and their six IMemoryCache implementations, the
    metering trio, the Serilog enrichers/sink, the GitHub markdown connectors, the role and
    team constants, and four enums. Every one kept its original namespace, so no `using`,
    `_ViewImports`, `<Using Include>` or analyzer full-name constant changed anywhere in the
    repo. The rename of this project to Humans.Base is a later lane.

    G5 lane 4b-iii A brought the non-Razor half of Humans.UI down here: Authorization/
    (PolicyNames, RoleChecks, ShiftRoleChecks), Constants/TempDataKeys, Controllers/
    (HumansControllerBase, ApiControllerBase), the eight Extensions/, Filters/
    ApiKeyAuthFilterBase, Helpers/LanguageCatalog, twelve Models/ view models and
    Models/Tables/. Namespaces are still Humans.UI.* on purpose — every `using`,
    `@using` and `_ViewImports` in the repo is unchanged. Humans.UI keeps everything a
    Razor file binds by ASSEMBLY name (tag helpers, view components, views, SharedResource
    and its resx set); lane B takes those with the SDK change.

    G5 lane 3a-2 followed with the AddSectionDbContext cluster: the generic registration seam
    (SectionDbContextServiceCollectionExtensions), SectionDbContextRegistration,
    SectionMigrationRunner, DatabaseMigrationHostedService, PreMigrationSnapshot,
    SectionMigrationsHistory, QueryMonitoringInterceptor and QueryStatistics. All 28
    table-owning sections call the seam from their own Section.cs and none of them may
    reference Humans.Infrastructure, so Base is the only place it can live. Namespaces
    preserved again (Humans.Infrastructure.Data / .Hosting), so no call site moved.
    InfrastructureServiceCollectionExtensions itself deliberately stayed in
    Humans.Infrastructure — two architecture rules read typeof(…).Assembly off it to mean
    "the Humans.Infrastructure assembly".
  -->

  <!--
    Followed the G5 lane 3a-1 arrivals out of Humans.Application: TrackedCache's
    MarkWarmedForTesting, LoggerTimingExtensions.SelectLogLevel and TrackedLock.WaitToLogLevel
    are internal and were already visible to these two test assemblies from there.
  -->
  <!--
    Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Web puts these in ImplicitUsings; Microsoft.NET.Sdk does not. Declaring
    them keeps the files G5 lane 4b-iii A moved out of Humans.UI (which declared the same items
    for the same reason) byte-identical to their originals — DateTimeDisplayExtensions names
    IHttpContextAccessor and ISession, HumansControllerBase names ILoggerFactory, and none of
    them carries a using of its own. Microsoft.AspNetCore.Routing, the third item Humans.UI
    declares, is not needed by anything that moved.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <Using Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.Http" />
    <Using Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Logging" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Humans.Application.Tests" />
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Humans.Users.Tests" />
    <!--
      Followed the G5 lane 3a-2 arrivals: DatabaseMigrationHostedService,
      SectionDbContextRegistration, SectionMigrationRunner and PreMigrationSnapshot are internal
      and stay that way — 26 .Contracts leaves inherit this assembly's public surface, and the
      migration plumbing is not part of it. Humans.Infrastructure still names two of them
      (AddHumansPersistence registers the hosted service, AdminDatabaseDiagnosticsRepository
      injects the registrations) and Humans.Integration.Tests drives SectionMigrationRunner
      directly; both were already inside Humans.Infrastructure's own InternalsVisibleTo set
      before the move.
    -->
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Humans.Infrastructure" />
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Humans.Integration.Tests" />
    <!--
      G5 lane 4b-iii A. HumanSearchPickerViewModel.SelectedBurnerName has an `internal`
      setter on purpose — no caller may supply a name, only the component may resolve one
      from SelectedUserId. The model came down here; its one privileged writer,
      HumanSearchViewComponent, is bound by ASSEMBLY name from Razor and so stays in
      Humans.UI until lane B. This entry preserves the invariant across that one-lane split.
      DELETE IT when Humans.UI dies (lane 5d) — the writer lands in this assembly.
    -->
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Humans.UI" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="NodaTime" />
    <!--
      ISection names IServiceCollection and IConfiguration, and the G5 lane 3a-1 arrivals
      below use IMemoryCache, ILogger, IOptions and IHostEnvironment. All live in the
      ASP.NET Core shared framework.

      Consequence worth knowing, measured 2026-08-15 with
      `dotnet msbuild <leaf>.csproj -t:ResolvePackageAssets -getItem:FrameworkReference`:
      FrameworkReference flows transitively through ProjectReference, so all 26 .Contracts
      leaves that reference this project resolve Microsoft.AspNetCore.App with
      IsTransitiveFrameworkReference=true. Those leaves are NOT framework-free and cannot be
      used as a "cannot name an ASP.NET type" oracle. Only the three leaves with no path here
      — Humans.Events.Contracts, Humans.Onboarding.Contracts (both zero-ProjectReference) and
      Humans.Users.Contracts (one edge, to Onboarding) — resolve Microsoft.NETCore.App alone.
    -->
    <FrameworkReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.App" />

    <!--
      Added by G5 lane 3a-1 (nobodies-collective/Humans#866), each forced by exactly one
      arrival. Keep this list minimal — 26 .Contracts leaves reference this project and
      inherit whatever lands here.
        CsvHelper — Csv/HumansCsv.cs, the single CSV seam (memory/code/csv-use-csvhelper.md).
        Serilog   — Logging/{CurrentUserEnricher,PiiRedactionEnricher,InMemoryLogSink}.cs,
                    which implement Serilog.Core.ILogEventEnricher / ILogEventSink.
        Octokit   — Services/GitHub{Guide,CommunityKb}ContentSource.cs, the Guide and
                    community-KB markdown readers.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="CsvHelper" />
    <PackageReference Include="Serilog" />
    <PackageReference Include="Octokit" />

    <!--
      Added by G5 lane 3a-2 (nobodies-collective/Humans#866) with the AddSectionDbContext
      cluster: the generic registration seam is called from all 28 table-owning sections, so it
      has to live below every one of them, and its body names DbContext, AddDbContext,
      AddDbContextFactory, IInterceptor, CoreEventId, UseNpgsql and UseNodaTime. This is the
      moment Base stops being EF-free — approved for that reason, not by drift. It changes no
      leaf's own reference set: the compiler emits an assembly reference only for what a project
      actually uses, and no .Contracts leaf names an EF type.
        Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore                    — DbContext, the options builders, the
                                                           interceptor base and the migration runner.
        Npgsql.EntityFrameworkCore.PostgreSQL            — UseNpgsql / NpgsqlDataSource.
        Npgsql.EntityFrameworkCore.PostgreSQL.NodaTime   — UseNodaTime on the Npgsql builder.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore" />
    <PackageReference Include="Npgsql.EntityFrameworkCore.PostgreSQL" />
    <PackageReference Include="Npgsql.EntityFrameworkCore.PostgreSQL.NodaTime" />

    <!--
      Added by G5 lane 4b-iii A with Extensions/HtmlHelperExtensions.cs, the single
      Html.SanitizedMarkdown seam that ~25 section views render through. Markdig parses,
      HtmlSanitizer scrubs; nothing else in this project names either package.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="HtmlSanitizer" />
    <PackageReference Include="Markdig" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    THE ONLY ProjectReference this project has, and it may not gain a second one that is
    not on a terminating chain — see the invariant at the top of this file.
    Forced by six arrivals from Humans.UI in G5 lane 4b-iii A: HumansControllerBase and
    ApiControllerBase (User, IUserService), RoleChecks, SearchResultMappingExtensions and
    PersonSearchOrderingExtensions.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Sections\Humans.Users.Contracts\Humans.Users.Contracts.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
